1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,325
<font color="#FFFFFF">Multilingual language models must decide how much
to train on each language,</font>

2
00:00:05,405 --> 00:00:09,197
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the standard answer has been temperature-based
sampling.</font>

3
00:00:09,277 --> 00:00:12,423
<font color="#FFFFFF">This paper from Google Research introduces UniMax,</font>

4
00:00:12,503 --> 00:00:18,231
<font color="#FFFFFF">a simple sampling method that gives more uniform
coverage to high-resource languages while</font>

5
00:00:18,311 --> 00:00:23,330
<font color="#FFFFFF">explicitly capping how many times any low-resource
language's data is repeated.</font>

6
00:00:23,410 --> 00:00:28,170
<font color="#FFFFFF">Across many benchmarks and model scales, UniMax
beats temperature sampling,</font>

7
00:00:28,250 --> 00:00:30,557
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the gains persist as models grow.</font>

8
00:00:30,637 --> 00:00:38,172
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors also release a refreshed mC4 corpus of
twenty-nine trillion characters across one hundred seven languages,</font>

9
00:00:38,252 --> 00:00:41,270
<font color="#FFFFFF">along with umT5 checkpoints trained with UniMax.</font>

10
00:00:41,650 --> 00:00:45,252
<font color="#FFFFFF">So why does language sampling matter so much?</font>

11
00:00:45,332 --> 00:00:48,934
<font color="#FFFFFF">Massively multilingual corpora are wildly imbalanced.</font>

12
00:00:49,014 --> 00:00:52,616
<font color="#FFFFFF">In the mC4 dataset, English alone has about nine
point seven trillion characters,</font>

13
00:00:52,696 --> 00:00:56,298
<font color="#FFFFFF">more than ninety-two thousand times the data
available for the lowest-resource language,</font>

14
00:00:56,378 --> 00:00:59,980
<font color="#FFFFFF">Yoruba.</font>

15
00:01:00,060 --> 00:01:03,662
<font color="#FFFFFF">If you train in proportion to the raw data,</font>

16
00:01:03,742 --> 00:01:07,344
<font color="#FFFFFF">the tail languages barely register at all.</font>

17
00:01:07,424 --> 00:01:11,026
<font color="#FFFFFF">Deciding how to balance them is an open and
expensive question,</font>

18
00:01:11,106 --> 00:01:14,708
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the field's default answer, temperature-based
sampling,</font>

19
00:01:14,788 --> 00:01:18,390
<font color="#FFFFFF">had never actually been evaluated systematically
across model scales.</font>

20
00:01:18,770 --> 00:01:24,024
<font color="#FFFFFF">Temperature sampling reshapes the data distribution
using a single exponent, tau.</font>

21
00:01:24,104 --> 00:01:25,407
<font color="#FFFFFF">But there is a catch.</font>

22
00:01:25,487 --> 00:01:29,293
<font color="#FFFFFF">When you tune tau to give the head languages more
presence,</font>

23
00:01:29,373 --> 00:01:31,598
<font color="#FFFFFF">you end up over-repeating the tail.</font>

24
00:01:31,678 --> 00:01:36,076
<font color="#FFFFFF">At tau equals three point three three, with a
trillion-token budget,</font>

25
00:01:36,156 --> 00:01:40,620
<font color="#FFFFFF">the lowest-resource languages get repeated more
than a hundred times.</font>

26
00:01:40,700 --> 00:01:45,098
<font color="#FFFFFF">That much repetition causes overfitting that hurts
downstream tasks,</font>

27
00:01:45,178 --> 00:01:48,325
<font color="#FFFFFF">it raises the risk of memorizing private content,</font>

28
00:01:48,405 --> 00:01:51,223
<font color="#FFFFFF">and it wastes compute on duplicate examples,</font>

29
00:01:51,303 --> 00:01:55,240
<font color="#FFFFFF">and every one of these harms only gets worse as
models scale.</font>

30
00:01:55,620 --> 00:01:59,327
<font color="#FFFFFF">UniMax makes four contributions.</font>

31
00:01:59,407 --> 00:02:03,113
<font color="#FFFFFF">First, the method itself,</font>

32
00:02:03,193 --> 00:02:06,900
<font color="#FFFFFF">which allocates the training budget uniformly while
capping how many times any single language repeats.</font>

33
00:02:06,980 --> 00:02:10,687
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second, an extensive ablation of sampling
strategies run across model scales,</font>

34
00:02:10,767 --> 00:02:14,473
<font color="#FFFFFF">the systematic study the field had been missing.</font>

35
00:02:14,553 --> 00:02:18,260
<font color="#FFFFFF">Third,</font>

36
00:02:18,340 --> 00:02:22,047
<font color="#FFFFFF">a refreshed mC4 corpus of twenty-nine trillion
characters spanning one hundred seven languages.</font>

37
00:02:22,127 --> 00:02:25,833
<font color="#FFFFFF">And fourth, released umT5 checkpoints trained with UniMax,</font>

38
00:02:25,913 --> 00:02:29,620
<font color="#FFFFFF">so practitioners can build on this work directly.</font>

39
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:33,594
<font color="#FFFFFF">Here is how UniMax works.</font>

40
00:02:33,674 --> 00:02:37,268
<font color="#FFFFFF">The name means uniform plus max.</font>

41
00:02:37,348 --> 00:02:40,943
<font color="#FFFFFF">You start from a fixed character budget, C,</font>

42
00:02:41,023 --> 00:02:44,617
<font color="#FFFFFF">and distribute it as uniformly as possible across
the languages,</font>

43
00:02:44,697 --> 00:02:48,291
<font color="#FFFFFF">processing them from lowest to highest resource.</font>

44
00:02:48,371 --> 00:02:51,965
<font color="#FFFFFF">At each step,</font>

45
00:02:52,045 --> 00:02:55,639
<font color="#FFFFFF">the algorithm checks whether the remaining
per-language budget can still be split evenly.</font>

46
00:02:55,719 --> 00:02:59,313
<font color="#FFFFFF">If a language would exceed N epochs over its own
corpus, it is capped at N epochs,</font>

47
00:02:59,393 --> 00:03:02,988
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the freed budget is redistributed uniformly
among the languages that remain.</font>

48
00:03:03,068 --> 00:03:06,662
<font color="#FFFFFF">The result is more uniform coverage of the head languages,</font>

49
00:03:06,742 --> 00:03:10,336
<font color="#FFFFFF">while no tail language is ever repeated more than N times.</font>

50
00:03:10,416 --> 00:03:14,010
<font color="#FFFFFF">With the default of N equals one, nothing repeats at all.</font>

51
00:03:14,390 --> 00:03:19,113
<font color="#FFFFFF">For evaluation, pretraining uses that refreshed mC4
corpus,</font>

52
00:03:19,193 --> 00:03:23,916
<font color="#FFFFFF">twenty-nine trillion characters across one hundred
seven languages.</font>

53
00:03:23,996 --> 00:03:28,719
<font color="#FFFFFF">The trained models are then tested on a broad suite
of tasks: TyDi QA GoldP,</font>

54
00:03:28,799 --> 00:03:33,521
<font color="#FFFFFF">WMT21 multilingual translation, XNLI, XQuAD, MLQA,
and PAWS-X.</font>

55
00:03:33,601 --> 00:03:38,324
<font color="#FFFFFF">Crucially,</font>

56
00:03:38,404 --> 00:03:43,127
<font color="#FFFFFF">every sampling strategy is compared while sweeping
the model scale from Small all the way up to XXL,</font>

57
00:03:43,207 --> 00:03:47,930
<font color="#FFFFFF">so the study can isolate whether the benefits
actually persist as the models get bigger.</font>

58
00:03:48,310 --> 00:03:51,153
<font color="#FFFFFF">And the results are remarkably consistent.</font>

59
00:03:51,233 --> 00:03:54,285
<font color="#FFFFFF">UniMax outperforms both temperature settings,</font>

60
00:03:54,365 --> 00:03:58,044
<font color="#FFFFFF">tau equals one and tau equals three point three three,</font>

61
00:03:58,124 --> 00:04:01,385
<font color="#FFFFFF">on average TyDi QA across all three model sizes,</font>

62
00:04:01,465 --> 00:04:06,257
<font color="#FFFFFF">and it beats temperature sampling on WMT21
translation at every scale,</font>

63
00:04:06,337 --> 00:04:09,459
<font color="#FFFFFF">with the majority of language pairs improving.</font>

64
00:04:09,539 --> 00:04:14,401
<font color="#FFFFFF">On high-resource languages it beats tau equals
three point three three,</font>

65
00:04:14,481 --> 00:04:18,368
<font color="#FFFFFF">and only trails tau equals one at the very largest scale.</font>

66
00:04:18,448 --> 00:04:21,361
<font color="#FFFFFF">On low-resource languages it wins outright,</font>

67
00:04:21,441 --> 00:04:28,530
<font color="#FFFFFF">even outperforming tau equals three point three
three on Swahili despite seeing fewer Swahili examples.</font>

68
00:04:28,910 --> 00:04:31,535
<font color="#FFFFFF">What about that max-epoch parameter, N?</font>

69
00:04:31,615 --> 00:04:33,338
<font color="#FFFFFF">Ablating N over one, five,</font>

70
00:04:33,418 --> 00:04:38,124
<font color="#FFFFFF">and ten on Large models gives TyDi QA scores of
eighty-two point two,</font>

71
00:04:38,204 --> 00:04:41,592
<font color="#FFFFFF">eighty-one point five, and eighty-one point eight.</font>

72
00:04:41,672 --> 00:04:45,684
<font color="#FFFFFF">Disallowing repeats entirely, N equals one, comes
out best,</font>

73
00:04:45,764 --> 00:04:47,556
<font color="#FFFFFF">though the effect is small.</font>

74
00:04:47,636 --> 00:04:52,273
<font color="#FFFFFF">At a four-times-larger budget, UniMax scores
eighty-three point one,</font>

75
00:04:52,353 --> 00:04:58,515
<font color="#FFFFFF">versus eighty-two point eight for temperature and
eighty-one point two for tau equals one.</font>

76
00:04:58,595 --> 00:05:04,757
<font color="#FFFFFF">The loss curves tell the story: high-temperature
overfitting grows more severe with scale,</font>

77
00:05:04,837 --> 00:05:06,560
<font color="#FFFFFF">while UniMax stays stable.</font>

78
00:05:06,940 --> 00:05:09,879
<font color="#FFFFFF">Let us put the headline numbers together.</font>

79
00:05:09,959 --> 00:05:16,432
<font color="#FFFFFF">The refreshed mC4 has twenty-nine trillion
characters across one hundred seven languages,</font>

80
00:05:16,512 --> 00:05:19,083
<font color="#FFFFFF">a thirty-five percent size increase.</font>

81
00:05:19,163 --> 00:05:24,238
<font color="#FFFFFF">On TyDi QA at the larger budget, UniMax scores
eighty-three point one,</font>

82
00:05:24,318 --> 00:05:26,005
<font color="#FFFFFF">ahead of both baselines.</font>

83
00:05:26,085 --> 00:05:30,497
<font color="#FFFFFF">The umT5 XXL checkpoint beats the earlier mT5 XXL
on TyDi QA.</font>

84
00:05:30,577 --> 00:05:34,326
<font color="#FFFFFF">And remember, at tau equals three point three three,</font>

85
00:05:34,406 --> 00:05:40,290
<font color="#FFFFFF">the lowest-resource languages were being repeated
more than a hundred times over.</font>

86
00:05:40,670 --> 00:05:42,100
<font color="#FFFFFF">The takeaway is simple.</font>

87
00:05:42,180 --> 00:05:47,155
<font color="#FFFFFF">Capping per-language repeats, and spreading the
rest of the budget uniformly,</font>

88
00:05:47,235 --> 00:05:50,832
<font color="#FFFFFF">beats temperature sampling for multilingual pretraining,</font>

89
00:05:50,912 --> 00:05:53,524
<font color="#FFFFFF">and that advantage holds as models scale.</font>

90
00:05:53,604 --> 00:05:58,710
<font color="#FFFFFF">UniMax is a drop-in, hyperparameter-light
replacement for temperature sampling,</font>

91
00:05:58,790 --> 00:06:03,700
<font color="#FFFFFF">and it ships with a refreshed corpus and umT5
checkpoints you can use today.</font>
